# The land (doc 62, phase 7): the country round about, its fields good and bad, rivers in flood, quarries, mines and
# the forest's edge, wild places and their stories, boundary stones and the quarrels over them, the view; and each
# trade's way of seeing it (soil, timber, ore, water, grazing, roads). Each region's own country:
# regions/land7_regions.talk. Alternatives run in parallel.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Anywhere

scene e5_la_boundary_stone
when topic=land
a : Somebody's moved the boundary stone again. Two feet, in the night. | There's a row about the boundary between the two farms out past the gate. | My grandfather beat the bounds every spring. Walked the whole parish line.
b[farmer] : Two feet a year and in forty years you've stolen a field. I know how it's done.
b[official] : There's a map in the town chest. Nobody ever looks at it.
b[child] : They beat the bounds? With sticks?
b : Shameful. | Always a row about something. | Every inch of it?
a : Shameful and slow. | It'll end in a fist fight. | Every inch. Twice.

scene e5_la_view
when topic=land here.weather=clear
a : You can see clear to the next hills today. | Look how far you can see. I never knew the land went on so. | On a day like this you could count every field in the parish.
b[old] : I've looked at that view every day of my life. It's never the same twice.
b[stage=young] : Can you see the sea from here? Can you?
b : It's a fine country. | It goes on further than that. | Go on, then. Count.
a : That it is. | Further than I'll ever walk. | Forty-one. And a half.

scene e5_la_flood
when topic=land
a : Do you remember when the river came over the banks? Water in the chapel. | The river's up after the rain. Brown and fast. | My father said the river flooded the year I was born. Took the mill wheel.
b[old] : Up to my knees in the parlour. We kept the hens on the table.
b[farmer] : A flood's a curse in spring and a blessing in autumn. Leaves the silt.
b : I'll never forget it. | Stay off the banks. | That's an omen, that.
a : Nor me. | I'll keep well back. | Of what? I've been fine.

scene e5_la_good_field
when topic=land
a : There's one field everyone wants and nobody can have. The one by the brook. | Old Tam's field never fails. Every year, a crop. | Some fields are lucky. Some are cursed. Ask anyone.
b[farmer] : Luck's got nothing to do with it. It's drainage and dung.
b[priest] : No field is cursed. Some are tended better.
b : Belongs to the Houses, I expect. | What does he do to it? | Which is cursed?
a : Everything does. | Says he sings to it. | The one with the dead tree.

scene e5_la_bad_field
when topic=land
a : That top field's never grown a decent crop in living memory. | Stones. That field grows stones. Every spring a new crop of them. | There's a field nobody will plough. Something buried there, they say.
b[farmer] : Stones come up with the frost. Pick them, build a wall. That's what walls are for.
b[young] : Treasure? Can we dig?
b : Let it go to grass. | Sell them. | Leave it be.
a : Sheep won't even eat it. | Who'd buy stones? | Gladly.

scene e5_la_wild_place
when topic=land
a : There's a hollow out past the woods nobody goes near after dark. | My gran said never to drink from the black pool past the old wall. | You know the stone circle on the hill? Something lives up there.
b[piety=devout] : Say a prayer if you pass it. Just in case.
b[humour=dry] : Something lives everywhere. Mostly rabbits.
b[young] : What lives there? Tell me!
b : Nor me. | Why not? | Just the wind.
a : Wise. | She never said. | So you say.

scene e5_la_ruins
when topic=land
a : There's an old wall out in the fields. Older than anyone. Nobody knows who built it. | Found an old coin in the furrow. Strange face on it. | There are ruins in the hills nobody's ever mapped.
b[scholar] : Older folk than us were here. We farm their bones.
b : Leave it be. | Spend it. | Leave them unmapped.
a : I do. | It won't spend. | Some things should be.

scene e5_la_paths
when topic=land
a : There's a path over the hill the old folk used. It's gone to bramble now. | Every path round here was made by somebody's cow. | The old track to the next valley's quicker than the road. If you know it.
b[herder] : That's the drove road. My grandfather took sheep along it.
b[carter] : Quicker on foot. Try it with a cart and see.
b : Nobody walks anymore. | Cows have more sense than surveyors. | Do you know it?
a : Nobody has time. | Much more. | I'll not tell.

scene e5_la_forest_edge
when topic=land
a : The woods are creeping closer to the fields every year. | You can hear wolves in the far woods some nights. Wild ones. Not like us. | The forest edge is where the old stories start.
b[woodcutter] : Creeping is right. Turn your back and there's a birch in your barley.
b[humour=dry] : Wild cousins. Don't invite them to supper.
b : Cut them back. | Gives me shivers. | And end.
a : Somebody should. | Me too. | Usually badly.

scene e5_la_quarry
when topic=land
a : The quarry's eaten half the hill since I was a pup. | Blasting at the quarry again. Rattled every cup in the house. | Where do you think all that stone goes?
b[laborer] : Somebody has to cut it. Pays better than the fields.
b[house=Greatly] : Into walls, roads and bridges. Into the realm.
b : It'll eat the rest. | Every week. | Ridgemere. Everything goes to Ridgemere.
a : And then us. | The cups are fine. Me, less so. | Like everything else.

scene e5_la_springs
when topic=land
a : There's a spring up the hill tastes better than any ale. | The well's water comes from under the hill. Cold even in summer. | Folk used to leave pennies at the spring for luck.
b[brewer] : That spring's why my ale's the best for miles.
b[young] : I left a button! Does that count?
b : Show me. | Clean water's a treasure. | Do they still?
a : I'll take you. | The best. | Some. I do.

scene e5_la_land_owner
when topic=land a.wealth=poor|destitute
a : Every field you can see belongs to somebody, and none of them to me. | My family farmed that ground for a hundred years. Never owned an inch of it. | The land's rich. The ones who work it aren't.
b[wealth=rich] : Ownership's a responsibility, you know. Not just a pleasure.
b[warmth=warm] : One day, maybe. Things change.
b : Same as ever. | It's not fair. | Never has been.
a : Some burden. | Not fast enough. | No.

scene e5_la_stars_fields
when topic=land here.time=night here.weather=clear
a : Out past the last lamp the fields go silver under the moon. | You can see the hills against the stars, if you let your eyes settle. | The land's different at night. Bigger.
b[guard] : Pretty from the wall. You still want to keep watching it.
b : Lovely. | Takes a while. | And quieter.
a : It is. | Worth the wait. | Mostly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- By trade

scene e5_la_farmer_soil
when topic=land a.job=farmer
a : Pick up a pawful of this. Feel it. That's good soil. | My soil's sour this year. Too much rain. | I can tell a field by its smell. That one's tired.
b[farmer] : Lime it. My father swore by lime.
b[child] : It's just dirt.
b : It's just dirt to me. | Will it come right? | Let it rest a year, then.
a : Dirt's the whole world, #address#. | With work. | Can't afford to.

scene e5_la_farmer_weeds
when topic=land a.job=farmer|laborer
a : Thistles. The more you pull, the more come. | The docks have taken the bottom field. Roots like ropes. | Weeds grow better than anything I plant.
b : Burn them out. | Pull them in spring. | Grow weeds and sell them.
a : I'd burn the field. | I did. | Who'd buy them?

scene e5_la_woodcutter_trees
when topic=land a.job=woodcutter
a : I know every big tree for a day's walk. Like old friends. | There's an oak out there older than any family in this town. I'll not cut it. | The young trees grow straighter where we've thinned.
b[child] : Do trees have names?
b : Friends you cut down. | Good of you. | Clever.
a : Some I don't. | Some trees should just be. | It's just sense.

scene e5_la_miner_hills
when topic=land a.job=miner
a : You walk over the hills. I walk through them. | I know where every vein runs under this land. Like a map in my head. | The hills are hollow under your feet. Mind that.
b : I prefer over. | Could you draw it? | Don't tell me that.
a : Each to their own. | Not for you. | Too late.

scene e5_la_fisher_water
when topic=land a.job=fisher
a : The water's the only land worth knowing. | The banks change every year. New pools, old pools gone. | I read the water like you'd read a book. If you could read.
b[fisher] : And the fish read us, just as well.
b : Don't fall in. | Must make work. | Cheek.
a : I've done it. Twice. | It keeps you sharp. | You love me.

scene e5_la_herder_grazing
when topic=land a.job=herder
a : The best grazing's always on the steep bit. Sheep know. | Moved the flock to the far pasture. The near one's bitten to the root. | Every bit of this land's been grazed for a thousand years. You can tell.
b[farmer] : Keep them off my barley, that's all I ask.
b : Sheep are cleverer than you'd think. | Long walk. | How?
a : Than most wolves. | Long enough. | The grass is sweet. Short and sweet.

scene e5_la_carter_roads
when topic=land a.job=carter
a : There's a bad stretch past the bridge. Lost a wheel there twice. | They should put a bridge over the ford. Half my days I'm waiting for the water to drop. | Every road round here was laid by somebody who never drove a cart.
b : Tell the Council. | Wade it. | Drive it better, then.
a : They don't drive carts either. | With a cart? | I drive it beautifully.

scene e5_la_hunting_land
when topic=land a.job=woodcutter|herder|farmer
a : Deer have been at the hedges. Tracks everywhere. | There's a fox earth under the bank. Got two of my hens. | Hares on the top field. You can see them boxing at dawn.
b[guard] : No poaching, now. You know the rule.
b : Venison, then. | Block it. | Lovely sight.
a : I didn't say anything. | Done. They dig. | It is.

scene e5_la_scholar_land
when topic=land a.job=scholar|priest|official
a : The old maps say this valley was a lake once. | The parish rolls go back two hundred years. Every field named. | Half these field names mean nothing now. "Hanging Wolf Acre". Imagine.
b[farmer] : Hanging Wolf Acre's still called that. And nobody likes ploughing it.
b : A lake? Here? | Who cares, two hundred years on? | I'd rather not.
a : Before the hills moved. | The lawyers. | Quite.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Who they are

scene e5_la_old_changes
when topic=land a.stage=old
a : That was all woods when I was a pup. All of it. | There used to be a pond there. Frogs all summer. Drained it for a field. | The river's moved. It used to come right up to that wall.
b[young] : All of it? Where did it go?
b : Hard to picture. | Pity. | Rivers do.
a : Into fires and roofs. | Frogs, though. | Like folk. Wandering off.

scene e5_la_young_explore
when topic=land a.stage=young
a : I walked all the way to the big rock and back! On my own! | There's a cave in the hill! Real one! | I found a stream nobody knows about.
b[parent=yes] : On your own? We'll talk about that.
b[stage=old] : I found that cave at your age. Mind the bats.
b : Clever pup. | Did you go in? | Show me, then.
a : Easy. | Nearly! | It's a secret.

scene e5_la_rich_land
when topic=land a.wealth=rich
a : We've bought the meadow past the mill. Good grazing. | Land's the only investment that doesn't run off with the steward. | I rode out to look at our holdings. More than I'd thought.
b[wealth=poor] : Must be nice to count fields like coins.
b : Congratulations. | Ha. True. | Lucky you.
a : It is. | Usually. | Prudence, not luck.

scene e5_la_stranger_directions
when topic=land pair.band=strangers
a : Where does that road go, past the hill? | Is there a quicker way to the river from here? | What's out beyond the last farm?
b[carter] : Every road goes somewhere. That one goes somewhere slow.
b : Next town, if you keep on long enough. | Through the fields, if the farmer doesn't catch you. | Nothing. Then more nothing.
a : Thank you. | I'll risk it. | Sounds restful.
